  "sport": "soccer",
  "title": "Drag it behind the standing leg",
  "description": "Two lines of cones about 10 metres apart. The players stand side by side on one line, each with a ball and their own lane. They dribble towards the other line and turn there by dragging the ball with the inside of the foot behind the standing leg – straight back or at an angle. Then they dribble back and turn the same way at the start line, back and forth several times.\n\nPractise the turn both ways. Once it works, add a feint just before the turn: a fake pass or shot, a step-over or a body feint. After the turn, speed up for a few metres.\n\nProgression: a partner stands on the turning line and steps forward like a defender. Turn when they get close, and try the turn in different directions, as in a match. The diagram is not to scale.",
  "coachingPoints": [
    {
      "id": "cp1",
      "text": "Plant the standing foot beside and slightly in front of the ball."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp2",
      "text": "Drag the ball with the inside of the foot behind the standing leg – ball and body turn together."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp3",
      "text": "Sell the feint: your eyes and arms should say you are passing or shooting."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp4",
      "text": "Explode out of the turn – it is the pace afterwards that gets you free."
    }
  ],
